Calc: an import gives back the spare capacity of the columns' cell blocks once the cells are in.

An import appends the cells of a column one by one, so each block of cells (and of cell text attributes) grows by
doubling and ends up to half empty: a sheet of 5.3 million text cells kept about 30-40 MB of unused block
capacity for as long as the document was open. ScDocumentImport::initColumn now cuts the blocks to their size
(mdds shrink_to_fit: the cells are moved, the pooled strings and formula cells stay the same objects). A later
edit that grows a block reallocates it once, as an edit of any full block does. The environment variable
HST_SC_IMPORT_SHRINK_OFF keeps the capacity as before.

New logic in sc/inc/hstcellstoreshrink.hxx (HST). Hook: documentimport.cxx (2 lines).

License: modified upstream files keep their license (MPL-2.0); the new header is HST-proprietary.
Test: CppunitTest_sc_ucalc_condformat testImportedColumnsStayEditableAfterShrink: an import with and without the
shrink, then 1000 rows inserted in the middle of the blocks, cells written into them and appended, gives the
same sheet.
---
--- /dev/null
+++ b/sc/inc/hstcellstoreshrink.hxx
@@ -0,0 +1,32 @@
+/* -*- Mode: C++; tab-width: 4; indent-tabs-mode: nil; c-basic-offset: 4 -*- */
